<http://example.org/kg/link/osberghaus_2012_kinetic_sma__osberghaus_2012_rapid_equilibrium_sma> rdfs:label "Osberghaus 2012 (kinetic SMA) ~ Osberghaus 2012 (rapid equilibrium SMA)" ;
    mkg:linkDescription "Osberghaus 2012 (kinetic SMA) and Osberghaus 2012 (rapid equilibrium SMA) describe protein binding and elution in strong cation-exchange chromatography, using the same proteins, SP Sepharose FF column, pH conditions, and comparison of gradient/breakthrough parameter estimation with inverse chromatogram fitting. Both model column transport, particle mass transfer, and steric mass action sorption, and report similar predictive performance and sensitivity to retention-time shifts. Osberghaus 2012 (kinetic SMA) is labeled “Kinetic SMA” and notes kinetic effects omitted from the isothermal fit; Osberghaus 2012 (rapid equilibrium SMA) explicitly describes the formulation as rapid-equilibrium SMA. Thus, they are closely related descriptions of the same study and system, with the distinction focused on the sorption formulation." ;
    mkg:provenance "written by gpt-6-luna from the two Sups' descriptions and facts, 2026-10-29 (A/B replaced by the model names by code)" ;
    mkg:similarity 0.959 .

<http://example.org/kg/link/seidel_2018_detailed_kinetic_model__seidel_2018_simplified_kinetic_model_dynamic> rdfs:label "Seidel 2018 (detailed kinetic model) ~ Seidel 2018 (simplified kinetic model dynamic)" ;
    mkg:linkDescription "Seidel 2018 (detailed kinetic model) and Seidel 2018 (simplified kinetic model dynamic) model methanol synthesis from CO, CO₂, and hydrogen over the same Cu/ZnO/Al₂O₃ catalyst, including CO and CO₂ hydrogenation and reverse water-gas shift. Both were fitted to the same 140 steady-state experiments and are intended for changing feed conditions. Seidel 2018 (detailed kinetic model) is a detailed, steady-state algebraic Langmuir–Hinshelwood model based on elementary surface steps; Seidel 2018 (simplified kinetic model dynamic) uses lumped kinetics and adds dynamic balances for reactor gas composition and the changing fraction of reduced catalyst centers. Seidel 2018 (simplified kinetic model dynamic) also captures feed-switching transients and improves identifiability relative to Seidel 2018 (detailed kinetic model)." ;
    mkg:provenance "written by gpt-6-luna from the two Sups' descriptions and facts, 2026-10-29 (A/B replaced by the model names by code)" ;
    mkg:similarity 0.928 .

<http://example.org/kg/link/seidel_2018_detailed_kinetic_model__seidel_2018_simplified_kinetic_model_steady_state> rdfs:label "Seidel 2018 (detailed kinetic model) ~ Seidel 2018 (simplified kinetic model steady state)" ;
    mkg:linkDescription "Seidel 2018 (detailed kinetic model) and Seidel 2018 (simplified kinetic model steady state) model methanol synthesis over Cu/ZnO/Al₂O₃, predicting CO and CO₂ hydrogenation and reverse water-gas shift rates for changing feed compositions. Both were fitted to the same 140 steady-state Micro-Berty experiments and achieved similar fits. Seidel 2018 (detailed kinetic model) uses a detailed Langmuir–Hinshelwood formulation based on elementary surface steps, while Seidel 2018 (simplified kinetic model steady state) uses lumped kinetics and calculates the oxidized/reduced surface-center fractions algebraically from an equilibrium morphology relation. Both steady-state formulations are algebraic; the switching experiments also showed that a dynamic surface-center variant of Seidel 2018 (simplified kinetic model steady state) better captured catalyst lag." ;
    mkg:provenance "written by gpt-6-luna from the two Sups' descriptions and facts, 2026-10-29 (A/B replaced by the model names by code)" ;
    mkg:similarity 0.914 .

<http://example.org/kg/link/seidel_2018_detailed_kinetic_model__vandenbussche_1996> rdfs:label "Seidel 2018 (detailed kinetic model) ~ Vanden Bussche 1996" ;
    mkg:linkDescription "Seidel 2018 (detailed kinetic model) and Vanden Bussche 1996 are mechanistic steady-state kinetic models of methanol synthesis and the reverse water-gas-shift reaction over commercial Cu/ZnO/Al₂O₃ catalysts, and both address how feed composition and operating conditions affect reaction behavior. They are worth comparing as alternative descriptions of this process; Seidel 2018 (detailed kinetic model)’s paper specifically compares its predictions with Vanden Bussche 1996. Seidel 2018 (detailed kinetic model) includes explicit CO hydrogenation as well as CO₂ hydrogenation, whereas Vanden Bussche 1996 couples methanol synthesis and reverse water-gas shift through a shared surface oxygen intermediate. Seidel 2018 (detailed kinetic model) was fitted to differential Micro-Berty reactor experiments using partial pressures, while Vanden Bussche 1996 was fitted to integral bench-reactor conversions and considered fugacities. Seidel 2018 (detailed kinetic model)’s paper reports that Vanden Bussche 1996 could not reproduce its pure-CO experiments and had larger deviations on other experiments." ;
    mkg:provenance "written by gpt-6-luna from the two Sups' descriptions and facts, 2026-10-29 (A/B replaced by the model names by code)" ;
    mkg:similarity 0.891 .

<http://example.org/kg/link/seidel_2018_simplified_kinetic_model_dynamic__seidel_2018_simplified_kinetic_model_steady_state> rdfs:label "Seidel 2018 (simplified kinetic model dynamic) ~ Seidel 2018 (simplified kinetic model steady state)" ;
    mkg:linkDescription "Seidel 2018 (simplified kinetic model dynamic) and Seidel 2018 (simplified kinetic model steady state) are variants of the same simplified methanol-synthesis model over a Cu/ZnO/Al₂O₃ catalyst, using lumped Langmuir–Hinshelwood kinetics for CO hydrogenation, CO₂ hydrogenation, and reverse water-gas shift. Both distinguish oxidized and reduced surface centers and were fitted using the same 140 steady-state experiments. Seidel 2018 (simplified kinetic model steady state) calculates the surface-center fraction algebraically from an equilibrium morphology relation, while Seidel 2018 (simplified kinetic model dynamic) adds dynamic catalyst-state and reactor balances to represent catalyst lag during feed switches. Thus, Seidel 2018 (simplified kinetic model dynamic) extends the quasi-static approach in Seidel 2018 (simplified kinetic model steady state) to capture transients; Seidel 2018 (simplified kinetic model steady state) focuses on steady-state prediction." ;
    mkg:provenance "written by gpt-6-luna from the two Sups' descriptions and facts, 2026-10-29 (A/B replaced by the model names by code)" ;
    mkg:similarity 0.938 .

<http://example.org/kg/link/seidel_2018_simplified_kinetic_model_dynamic__vandenbussche_1996> rdfs:label "Seidel 2018 (simplified kinetic model dynamic) ~ Vanden Bussche 1996" ;
    mkg:linkDescription "Seidel 2018 (simplified kinetic model dynamic) and Vanden Bussche 1996 model methanol synthesis and the reverse water-gas shift over commercial Cu/ZnO/Al₂O₃ catalysts, predicting how reaction conditions affect methanol production and gas composition. Vanden Bussche 1996 is a steady-state mechanistic model with a detailed surface-reaction scheme, fitted using integral bench-reactor experiments; Seidel 2018 (simplified kinetic model dynamic) uses simplified, lumped kinetics and adds dynamic catalyst-state and reactor balances to represent feed-switching transients. Seidel 2018 (simplified kinetic model dynamic) also includes CO hydrogenation, which the paper says Vanden Bussche 1996 neglects and which prevented Vanden Bussche 1996 from reproducing pure-CO-feed experiments. Their reactor configurations and purposes differ: Vanden Bussche 1996 examines steady-state reactor behavior, while Seidel 2018 (simplified kinetic model dynamic) is aimed at dynamic simulation under changing feeds." ;
    mkg:provenance "written by gpt-6-luna from the two Sups' descriptions and facts, 2026-10-29 (A/B replaced by the model names by code)" ;
    mkg:similarity 0.86 .

<http://example.org/kg/link/seidel_2018_simplified_kinetic_model_steady_state__vandenbussche_1996> rdfs:label "Seidel 2018 (simplified kinetic model steady state) ~ Vanden Bussche 1996" ;
    mkg:linkDescription "Seidel 2018 (simplified kinetic model steady state) and Vanden Bussche 1996 both model methanol synthesis and the reverse water-gas-shift reaction over Cu/ZnO/Al₂O₃ catalysts, and both predict reaction behavior across different feed compositions. Seidel 2018 (simplified kinetic model steady state) is a simplified, lumped Langmuir–Hinshelwood model with composition-dependent oxidized and reduced surface centers; Vanden Bussche 1996 uses a more detailed surface reaction scheme in which the reactions share an oxygen intermediate. Seidel 2018 (simplified kinetic model steady state) was fitted to differential-reactor experiments and includes CO hydrogenation, which the paper says allows it to reproduce pure-CO experiments that Vanden Bussche 1996 could not. Vanden Bussche 1996 was fitted to integral bench-reactor data and couples its kinetics to reactor material balances; Seidel 2018 (simplified kinetic model steady state)’s authors also assess its models for changing feeds, including dynamic surface-center behavior." ;
    mkg:provenance "written by gpt-6-luna from the two Sups' descriptions and facts, 2026-10-29 (A/B replaced by the model names by code)" ;
    mkg:similarity 0.872 .

<http://example.org/kg/link/wang_2019__xing_2010> rdfs:label "Wang 2019 ~ Xing 2010" ;
    mkg:linkDescription "Wang 2019 and Xing 2010 are dynamic kinetic models of biological cultivation in bioreactors, each using six coupled equations to predict culture time courses. Wang 2019 models light- and nitrogen-dependent growth and carbon allocation in *Dunaliella viridis* in a flat-panel photobioreactor; Xing 2010 models nutrient-limited growth, metabolite effects, and B1 production in fed-batch CHO cultures. Wang 2019 focuses on photosynthesis, storage compounds, and chlorophyll under changing light and nitrogen, while Xing 2010 focuses on cells, nutrients, metabolites, and antibody-fusion-protein production under daily feeding. Both were calibrated against culture data and tested on separate experiments, but they describe different organisms and cultivation processes." ;
    mkg:provenance "written by gpt-6-luna from the two Sups' descriptions and facts, 2026-10-29 (A/B replaced by the model names by code)" ;
    mkg:similarity 0.7 .

<http://example.org/kg/link/xing_2010__zentou_2019_andrews> rdfs:label "Xing 2010 ~ Zentou 2019 (Andrews)" ;
    mkg:linkDescription "Xing 2010 and Zentou 2019 (Andrews) are dynamic bioreactor-culture models that predict biomass, substrate-related quantities, and product formation, and both represent inhibition affecting cell growth. Xing 2010 models fed-batch CHO production of a B1 fusion protein, with nutrient limitation and inhibition by ammonia and lactate. Zentou 2019 (Andrews) models batch yeast fermentation for ethanol, using the Andrews law to represent inhibition by excess substrate and yield coefficients to link growth, substrate consumption, and ethanol production. They therefore offer comparable growth-and-production modeling approaches, but differ in organism, operating mode, products, and the source of inhibition represented." ;
    mkg:provenance "written by gpt-6-luna from the two Sups' descriptions and facts, 2026-10-29 (A/B replaced by the model names by code)" ;
    mkg:similarity 0.663 .

<http://example.org/kg/link/xing_2010__zentou_2019_monod> rdfs:label "Xing 2010 ~ Zentou 2019 (Monod)" ;
    mkg:linkDescription "Xing 2010 and Zentou 2019 (Monod) are lumped dynamic culture models that represent substrate-limited cell growth and track biomass or cell density alongside extracellular concentrations and product. Xing 2010 describes fed-batch CHO production of a B1 protein, with six predicted variables and additional ammonia and lactate inhibition mechanisms. Zentou 2019 (Monod) describes batch molasses fermentation by S. cerevisiae, linking biomass growth to sugar consumption and ethanol production through yield coefficients. Their shared Monod-style growth basis makes them comparable, while their organisms, products, operating modes, and model complexity differ." ;
    mkg:provenance "written by gpt-6-luna from the two Sups' descriptions and facts, 2026-10-29 (A/B replaced by the model names by code)" ;
    mkg:similarity 0.728 .

<http://example.org/kg/link/zentou_2019_andrews__zentou_2019_monod> rdfs:label "Zentou 2019 (Andrews) ~ Zentou 2019 (Monod)" ;
    mkg:linkDescription "Zentou 2019 (Andrews) and Zentou 2019 (Monod) are directly compared models of batch molasses fermentation by Saccharomyces cerevisiae, predicting biomass, substrate, and ethanol using growth-linked balances and yield coefficients. They share the same process and purpose, but Zentou 2019 (Andrews) uses an Andrews growth law that represents inhibition at high substrate concentrations, while Zentou 2019 (Monod) uses a Monod law that does not. Zentou 2019 (Andrews)’s parameters were estimated from higher initial substrate concentrations and it performs better at high concentrations; Zentou 2019 (Monod)’s were estimated at low concentrations and it performs better at low concentrations. They are rival formulations intended for use over different operating ranges." ;
    mkg:provenance "written by gpt-6-luna from the two Sups' descriptions and facts, 2026-10-29 (A/B replaced by the model names by code)" ;
    mkg:similarity 0.892 .

